<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article article-type="letter"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Br. J. Cancer</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10206283</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2362784</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6690274</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.bjc.6690274</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Short Communication</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Overexpression of metastasis-associated <italic>MTA1</italic> mRNA in invasive oesophageal carcinomas</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Toh</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Kuwano</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Mori</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Nicolson</surname><given-names>G L</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff4">4</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Sugimachi</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1">Division of Pathology, Clinical Research Institute, National Kyushu Cancer Center, Fukuoka, 811-1395, Japan</aff><aff id="aff2">Department of Surgery II, Faculty of Medicine, Kyushu University, Fukuoka, 812-8582, Japan</aff><aff id="aff3">Department of Surgery, Medical Institute of Bioregulation, Kyushu University, Beppu, 874-0838, Japan</aff><aff id="aff4">The Institute for Molecular Medicine, Huntington Beach, CA, 92649, USA</aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="caf1"><label>*</label>Division of Pathology, Clinical Research Institute, National Kyushu Cancer Center, 3-1-1 Notame, Minami-ku, Fukuoka 811-1395, Japan</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>4</month><year>1999</year></pub-date><volume>79</volume><issue>11-12</issue><fpage>1723</fpage><lpage>1726</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>16</day><month>04</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>10</day><month>06</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>13</day><month>07</month><year>1998</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1999</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>The <italic>MTA1</italic> gene is a recently identified novel candidate breast cancer metastasis-associated gene which has been implicated in the signal transduction or regulation of gene expression. We examined the mRNA expression levels of the <italic>MTA1</italic>, the human homologue of the rat <italic>mta1</italic> gene in 47 surgically resected oesophageal squamous cell carcinomas by quantitative reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction. The relative overexpression of <italic>MTA1</italic> mRNA (tumour/normal ratio &#x02265; 2) was observed in 16 out of 47 (34.0&#x00025;) oesophageal carcinomas. Oesophageal tumours overexpressing <italic>MTA1</italic> mRNA (T/N ratio &#x02265; 2) showed significantly higher frequencies of adventitial invasion (<italic>P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05) and lymph node metastasis (<italic>P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.05), and tended to have a higher rate of lymphatic involvement than the remaining tumours. Thus, the data suggest that the <italic>MTA1</italic> gene might play an important role in invasion and metastasis of oesophageal carcinomas. &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd><italic>MTA1</italic></kwd><kwd>oesophageal carcinoma</kwd><kwd>invasion</kwd><kwd>metastasis</kwd><kwd>reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>